<Project>

  <!--
    Merge PLib into the mod assembly. Without this the mod loads and then throws
    FileNotFoundException on PLib at first use, because ONI does not probe the
    mod folder for sibling assemblies.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL, rather than the target living in the .csproj:

    ILRepack.Lib.MSBuild.Task ships its own target, and it fires
    AfterTargets="Build" whenever $(Configuration) contains "Release". That
    default merges IN PLACE, globs every .dll in the output folder as an input,
    and passes no LibraryPath. Running it alongside a hand written merge target
    means the mod assembly gets repacked twice per build: the second pass reads
    an assembly whose PLib is already internalised and dies with

      Failed to resolve assembly: 'Newtonsoft.Json, Version=7.0.0.0'

    because it has no LibraryPath pointing at the game's copy. The first build
    after a clean still succeeds, so the failure only shows up on rebuild.

    The package's own opt out is this file. From its .targets:

      ILRepackTargetsFile   = $(ProjectDir)ILRepack.targets
      Import Project="$(ILRepackTargetsFile)" Condition="Exists(...)"
      Target Name="ILRepack" Condition="... and !Exists('$(ILRepackTargetsFile)')"

    So the moment this file exists the package imports it and skips both its
    default merge and its CleanReferenceCopyLocalPaths companion. There is
    exactly one merge per build again.

    Two further details, both deliberate:

    - The output is a merged\ subfolder, NOT $(TargetPath). Merging in place
      would overwrite the compiler's output with an assembly that already has
      PLib internalised, so the next build would feed an already merged assembly
      back into ILRepack. Keeping them apart means every merge starts from the
      same clean pair, and the target is safe to run on every build.
    - $(MergedAssembly) is set INSIDE the target. $(TargetDir) and
      $(TargetFileName) come from the SDK targets, which are imported after the
      project body, so at evaluation time "$(TargetDir)merged\" would collapse to
      the bare relative "merged\" and ILRepack would resolve it against the
      project directory.

    LibraryPath is the game's Managed folder because PLib references
    Newtonsoft.Json 7.0.0.0, the version the game ships; without it ILRepack
    cannot resolve that reference.
  -->
  <Target Name="MergePLib" AfterTargets="Build">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <MergedAssembly>$(TargetDir)merged\$(TargetFileName)</MergedAssembly>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <MakeDir Directories="$(TargetDir)merged" />
    <ItemGroup>
      <MergeInputs Include="$(TargetPath)" />
      <MergeInputs Include="$(TargetDir)PLib.dll" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <ILRepack
      Parallel="true"
      Internalize="true"
      InputAssemblies="@(MergeInputs)"
      TargetKind="Dll"
      LibraryPath="$(GameLibsDir)"
      OutputFile="$(MergedAssembly)" />
    <Message Importance="high" Text="PLib merged into $(MergedAssembly)" />
  </Target>

</Project>
